{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "The Big Argument", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Apologizing", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 8, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.3", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe complicated or unusual events in the past through a wider range of B1 language in both work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_8"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Modals For Possibility, Certainty, Suggestions and Expectations", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use modals when you want to make a guess about something. You choose different modals depending on how sure you are.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">You use may, might and could to express possibility in the past and in the future.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "*Might suggests that something is less possible than may", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<ul><li>might / might not + infinitive</li><li>could / could not + infinitive</li><li>may / may not + infinitive</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br><span class=\"italic\">This <span class=\"strong\">might</span> be the key to their apartment.<br/>You <span class=\"strong\">may</span> be able to get extra time to complete the examination.<br/>This <span class=\"strong\">could</span> be the answer to all our problems.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<br/><span class=\"strong\">Expressing Certainty</span><br>You use<span class=\"strong\"> must </span>to express that something is necessarily true.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Example: This <span class=\"strong\">must</span> be a mistake!</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Should, could, might and shall are used to express suggestions:</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Shouldn't</span> we have a get together before you head back to college? </span><br><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">could</span> talk to Richard about your problems at work.</span><br><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">might</span> consider taking the subway instead of driving.</span><br><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Shall</span> we meet at the restaurant before the movie?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<br/><span class=\"strong\">Will </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use will when you are very sure.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Example: <span class=\"strong\">She'll</span> be at the hotel by 6PM.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "It may also restore a sense of trust between two people.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Excessive apologizing may make us appear to lack in confidence and competence. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "This could be particularly damaging in workplace environments.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Negative Questions To Encourage Agreement", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Negative questions have a different meaning than regular questions. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use negative questions to confirm information you already know.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Negative questions can have two different meanings. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You can ask a negative question for confirmation of a positive belief.<br/>You can ask a negative question for confirmation of a negative belief.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Isn't it better to fight against self-criticism instead of apologizing all the time?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Wouldn't it be more productive to only apologize for behaviors that negatively affect other people?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Aren't they going to clean up the mess? ", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "resident", "type": "name"}, {"content": "working regularly at a particular place", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  Dr.McMillan, our resident psychologist is on the line now.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you would hear in a radio conversation.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "to restore", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to put or bring something back into existence ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It may also restore a sense of trust between two people. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "social cues", "type": "name"}, {"content": "signals people send through body language and expressions.", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Psychologists often suggest that we are supposed to make errors in judgement to learn social cues. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "mindset", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a particular way of thinking", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Over-apologizing could be a result of our own negative mindset. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "self-criticism", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to be critical about your own thoughts, beliefs or image", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  Isn't it better to fight against self-criticism?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "commercial", "type": "name"}, {"content": "paid for by advertisers", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We're taking calls after this commercial break. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "perspective", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a way of thinking about and understanding something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: That's an interesting perspective, Dr.McMillan.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud08", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "when it boils down to it", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something reduced to its important parts", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  When it boils down to it, you apologize too much!", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le01ob08aud07", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Write a 12-14 line dialogue between you and your friend on the telephone.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Extend an apology.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Explain how you felt when you realized you had forgotten their birthday.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use the modals <span class=\"italic\">may</span> and <span class=\"italic\">might</span>.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Explain what you should have done. <span class=\"italic\">Example: I should have marked your birthday on the calendar.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Encourage agreement. <span class=\"italic\">Example: I was pretty inconsiderate, wasn't I?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Proofread the dialogue.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Read the dialogue aloud with a partner.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I write a dialogue about an apology.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I describe how I felt when I forgot an important celebration.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write about what I should have done.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "_data": [], "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"image": "engenn8le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "time": "0", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"image": "engenn8le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "time": "100", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong notdialogue\">Radio Host</span>: Are you someone like me who apologizes for the things they have no control over? Dr. McMillan, our resident psychologist is on the line now to talk to you about what we should and should not be apologizing for."}, {"image": "engenn8le01ob08re2img02.jpg", "time": "27000", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong notdialogue\">Dr. McMillan</span>: Thanks, Bill.<br/><br/>Don't you think it would be better if we weren't so hard on ourselves?<br/><br/>Unless you are perfect, apologies are a necessary part of life.\nThose two little words - \u201cI'm sorry\u201d- can be quite powerful. They can often make us feel better.<br/><br/>Apologizing helps to release guilt, doesn't it? As we all know, saying \u201cI'm sorry\u201d improves relationships when we have wronged, annoyed or disappointed someone. It may also restore a sense of trust between two people."}, {"image": "engenn8le01ob08re2img03.jpg", "time": "58000", "id": 3, "text": "Why do some people then, apologize for things and situations outside our control like someone else's behavior? Unfortunately, excessive apologizing may make us appear to lack in confidence or competence. This could be particularly damaging in workplace environments.<br/><br/>Wouldn't it be more productive to only apologize for behaviors that negatively affect other people?"}, {"image": "engenn8le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "time": "86000", "id": 4, "text": "Over-apologizing could be a result of our own negative mindset. Often, we are too hard on ourselves rather than recognize that everyone makes mistakes. Psychologists often suggest that we are supposed to make errors in judgement to learn social cues and expectations.<br/><br/>When it boils down to it - isn't it better to fight against self-criticism instead of apologizing all the time?<br/><br/><span class=\"strong\">Radio Host</span>: An interesting perspective, Dr. McMillan.<br/><br/>We're taking calls and e-mails after this commercial break. Should we be reconsidering our use of the words ... I'm sorry?"}], "audiosrc": "engenn8le01ob08re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn8le01ob08re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "id": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "video", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"image": "", "time": "0", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"image": "", "time": "12000", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"bullet\">What are rhetorical questions?</span>"}, {"image": "", "time": "25000", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"bullet\">When do you use negative questions?</span>"}], "video_src": "engenn8le01ob08re3vid01"}, "type": "video"}], "id": 2}], "exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": " I can write about feelings and sensations and encourage agreement.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can write about things I am supposed to do and things that are supposed to happen.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can write rhetorical questions to encourage agreement. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Do you find it easy or difficult to apologize to others? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "When was the last time you apologized to someone? Why? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content":